[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Mocking and Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unit testing is an essential practice in modern software development. However, testing classes that depend on external systems, databases, or complex services can be challenging. This is where mocking comes into play."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Mocking?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mocking is the process of creating simulated objects (mocks) that mimic the behavior of real dependencies in controlled ways. Instead of hitting a real database or calling a live API, your unit test interacts with a mock object that returns predefined responses."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolate the code under test by removing external dependencies."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Speed up test execution significantly."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simulate difficult scenarios such as network failures or database exceptions easily."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Mockito"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mockito is the most popular mocking framework for Java. It allows you to create, verify, and stub mock objects with a clean and intuitive API. It integrates seamlessly with testing frameworks like JUnit."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Example of Using Mockito"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where a 'UserService' depends on a 'UserRepository'. We want to test 'UserService' without actually querying the database."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;
import static org.junit.Assert.*;
import org.junit.Test;

public class UserServiceTest {

    @Test
    public void testFindUser() {
        // 1. Create a mock of UserRepository
        UserRepository mockRepo = mock(UserRepository.class);

        // 2. Stub the method call to return a specific object
        when(mockRepo.findById(1)).thenReturn(new User(1, "John Doe"));

        // 3. Inject the mock into the service
        UserService service = new UserService(mockRepo);

        // 4. Execute business logic
        User user = service.getUser(1);

        // 5. Assert the results
        assertEquals("John Doe", user.getName());

        // 6. Verify interaction
        verify(mockRepo).findById(1);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mockito simplifies the creation of robust unit tests by allowing developers to focus purely on the logic of the unit under test. By mastering mock creation, stubbing, and verification, you can write fast, reliable, and isolated tests."}]
